Subject: Port: simulation profiles, so releases can play each other online
Upstream-base: 8b9d92dd88b4c727cc6359ee4cd5ba128cced7b8

The releases' game code simulates matches differently: the ball's bounce
and air resistance, the goalie's ball steals, the Mega Strike freeze, item
odds, the Yoshi egg's push, a Thwomp's tilt and more. A USA and a European
machine fed the same inputs therefore go out of sync. Each game module now
runs its own release's rules and the European revision 2's, the canonical
profile (include/mscharged_sim_profile.h, the port's
src/platform/sim_profile.cpp); a network match picks one for both machines
(tweak patch 0058), and every other game keeps the module's own. The
European module has only that profile and is unchanged.

In the modules of two profiles (MSCHARGED_SIM_RUNTIME: the USA's, Japan's
and Korea's), every #if of the simulation where their code and
revision 2's differ gets a block next to it, outside the #if, that asks
MSCHARGED_SIM_IS: a condition before the original's own code, which then
runs by its rules only, or revision 2's code where the module has none.

The ball's checksum fields differ too (mbBallFrozen is only the USA's), and
the game registers a checksum type once per run: with two profiles, each
gets its own type, so a USA machine on the European rules checksums what a
European machine does.

Left as they are, the sites that only draw (the hammer's landing pose, the
render snapshot), set fields the other rules never read (mbBallFrozen's and
the Yoshi egg's resets, a patch's spawn position, the ball's freeze, which
only the USA's Mega Strike sets) or already agree natively (the goalie's
deke launch scale).

The Japanese module shares the USA's sites. The Korean one has those and
its own, where it alone differs from every other release
(MSCHARGED_SIM_KOREA_RUNTIME): its goalie tests whether the ball heads
between the posts (IsPointInCone) with code of its own; it reaches farther
for a loose ball and chases it by a global of its own instead of the team's
skill tweak; its lob saves take other angles and fall back to a save; it
pursues a skill shot's shooter only so far and gives up on a deke; and its
ball hits players and goalies again when it leaves or reaches a tangible
player. On another profile, a block at each site runs the other releases'
code instead: the Korean code only on the Korean profile.

